WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.585 --> 00:00:04.630
[foreboding music]

2
00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:09.468
- By the summer of 1944.

3
00:00:09.468 --> 00:00:11.720
the terror and tyranny
of the Nazi Third Reich

4
00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:14.306
shrouds most of Europe.

5
00:00:14.306 --> 00:00:16.975
Occupied countries
resist as they can.

6
00:00:17.768 --> 00:00:20.938
But with the advance of
Soviet forces in the east

7
00:00:20.938 --> 00:00:24.441
and the success of the
Normandy invasion in the west.

8
00:00:24.441 --> 00:00:25.817
two of the world's great cities

9
00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:28.987
organize in force
and fight back.

10
00:00:29.488 --> 00:00:34.785
Even in the place of greatest
horror. there is resistance.

11
00:00:37.871 --> 00:00:39.373
- All wars change the world.

12
00:00:39.373 --> 00:00:41.208
but none of them
changed the world

13
00:00:41.208 --> 00:00:42.876
like the Second World War did.

14
00:00:43.585 --> 00:00:45.003
- Japan's on the march.

15
00:00:45.003 --> 00:00:46.713
Germany's on the march.

16
00:00:48.257 --> 00:00:49.424
- No one can imagine
the nightmare

17
00:00:49.424 --> 00:00:51.093
they're about to unleash.

18
00:00:51.635 --> 00:00:54.596
The most destructive
war in human history.

19
00:00:54.596 --> 00:00:57.474
- Suddenly. the world
is turned upside down

20
00:00:57.474 --> 00:00:59.268
and all hell is let loose.

21
00:01:01.228 --> 00:01:04.230
- The West is stunned by
the speed of the advance.

22
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.569
- You get the Allies
led by the big three:

23
00:01:08.569 --> 00:01:11.280
Roosevelt. Churchill. Stalin.

24
00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:16.243
Men who are dealing with
immensely complicated questions.

25
00:01:16.368 --> 00:01:20.706
- It's the biggest military
operation of human history.

26
00:01:20.706 --> 00:01:22.249
- The Allies have
to come together.

27
00:01:22.249 --> 00:01:25.043
not just militarily.
but industrial scale.

28
00:01:25.043 --> 00:01:27.045
It's a global perspective.

29
00:01:27.045 --> 00:01:28.922
- They have to fight in
every climate

30
00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:32.175
from the Arctic to the
jungles of the Pacific.

31
00:01:32.175 --> 00:01:35.429
to the deserts of Africa
and the depths of the ocean.

32
00:01:37.306 --> 00:01:38.599
[dramatic music]

33
00:01:38.599 --> 00:01:41.685
- But there was no
certainty of victory.

34
00:01:41.685 --> 00:01:44.896
It was going to be a
horrific bloodbath.

35
00:01:44.896 --> 00:01:47.190
- We see humans at
their absolute worst.

36
00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:49.443
how they treat
other human beings.

37
00:01:49.443 --> 00:01:51.278
and we see them at
their absolute best.

38
00:01:51.278 --> 00:01:53.989
willing to give their lives
that others might live.

39
00:01:53.989 --> 00:01:55.991
- World War II was a struggle

40
00:01:55.991 --> 00:01:59.077
in which there could
be one victor

41
00:01:59.077 --> 00:02:00.787
and one vanquished.

42
00:02:00.787 --> 00:02:03.665
[dramatic music]

43
00:02:07.544 --> 00:02:09.963
[sirens wail]

44
00:02:17.971 --> 00:02:20.807
[dramatic music]

45
00:02:27.314 --> 00:02:31.026
[broadcaster speaks French]

46
00:02:33.236 --> 00:02:34.738
- [Tom] Since the
fall of France.

47
00:02:34.738 --> 00:02:36.198
the BBC has broadcast

48
00:02:36.198 --> 00:02:38.950
daily radio programming
to occupied Europe.

49
00:02:40.202 --> 00:02:44.331
Embedded within the
broadcasts are coded messages.

50
00:02:44.331 --> 00:02:48.960
[broadcaster speaks French]

51
00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.629
- [Tom] The night before D-Day.

52
00:02:50.629 --> 00:02:53.131
the Allied invasion
of Western Europe.

53
00:02:53.131 --> 00:02:55.008
the broadcast to occupied France

54
00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:57.719
includes a line from a
poem by Paul Verlaine.

55
00:02:59.346 --> 00:03:02.849
[broadcaster speaks French]

56
00:03:06.144 --> 00:03:08.188
- The French
resistance is waiting.

57
00:03:08.188 --> 00:03:09.606
and when the BBC announces:

58
00:03:09.606 --> 00:03:12.234
"Wound my heart with
a monotonous languor."

59
00:03:12.234 --> 00:03:14.319
this is the word that they
have been waiting to hear.

60
00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:16.947
The invasion is on.

61
00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:21.868
- The Allies want that
resistance movement to rise up.

62
00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:23.870
to blow up German railroads.

63
00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:25.664
to ambush German patrols.

64
00:03:25.664 --> 00:03:28.875
and to disrupt the Germans
in any way they can.

65
00:03:30.252 --> 00:03:31.628
- [Col. Douds] All of their
planning.

66
00:03:31.628 --> 00:03:33.755
all of their networking
now goes into action

67
00:03:33.755 --> 00:03:36.633
to slow the German response

68
00:03:36.633 --> 00:03:37.884
so that the Allies
can win the race

69
00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:39.136
to build up and break out.

70
00:03:40.011 --> 00:03:42.722
- [Tom] So many men and
women throughout France

71
00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:45.642
have risked so much
to get to this night

72
00:03:46.643 --> 00:03:49.271
and the beginning of their
country's liberation.

73
00:03:52.482 --> 00:03:54.443
- [Robert] After the fall
of France in 1940.

74
00:03:54.443 --> 00:03:55.652
there are two Frances.

75
00:03:55.652 --> 00:03:57.362
There's the France

76
00:03:57.362 --> 00:04:00.115
that is directly occupied by
the Germans. including Paris.

77
00:04:00.115 --> 00:04:03.952
and there is a puppet regime
with its capital at Vichy.

78
00:04:03.952 --> 00:04:06.705
So resistance groups
form in each one.

79
00:04:09.332 --> 00:04:10.834
- [Tom] From the start.

80
00:04:10.834 --> 00:04:13.587
the inspiration for French
resistance to Germany

81
00:04:13.587 --> 00:04:15.213
was embodied by one man.

82
00:04:18.507 --> 00:04:22.512
A veteran of the Great War.
General Charles De Gaulle.

83
00:04:23.555 --> 00:04:25.682
- [Col. Douds] Charles De Gaulle
fought all the way through

84
00:04:25.682 --> 00:04:28.393
the German invasion
of France in 1940.

85
00:04:29.436 --> 00:04:30.812
- Rather than surrender
to the Germans.

86
00:04:30.812 --> 00:04:32.481
rather than go along with
the French government.

87
00:04:32.481 --> 00:04:35.317
he arrives in Britain by
himself and just says.

88
00:04:35.317 --> 00:04:37.402
"By the way. I'm now the
leader of the Free French."

89
00:04:37.402 --> 00:04:38.695
He's self-appointed.

90
00:04:39.196 --> 00:04:42.282
Churchill goes. "Eh.
he might be useful."

91
00:04:42.282 --> 00:04:44.242
He gives him an office.
gives him a radio.

92
00:04:44.242 --> 00:04:45.744
lets him do some broadcasts.

93
00:04:46.411 --> 00:04:50.415
[Charles speaks French]

94
00:04:54.044 --> 00:04:55.212
- [Col. Douds] He's gonna
announce:

95
00:04:55.337 --> 00:04:56.838
"The Vichy government
is illegitimate."

96
00:04:56.838 --> 00:04:58.882
"Anybody who's in France
should come over to England.

97
00:04:58.882 --> 00:05:00.509
and we're gonna start
to put together an army

98
00:05:00.509 --> 00:05:02.052
that's gonna
liberate ourselves.

99
00:05:02.052 --> 00:05:03.553
If you can't get here.

100
00:05:03.553 --> 00:05:05.430
you should start resisting
while you're there."

101
00:05:07.015 --> 00:05:08.809
- [Robert] This is an extremely
important event

102
00:05:08.809 --> 00:05:10.769
in galvanizing
the French people.

103
00:05:11.686 --> 00:05:14.189
Up to that point. they'd been
in a state of despondency

104
00:05:14.189 --> 00:05:17.234
at the rapid defeat at
the hands of the Germans.

105
00:05:17.734 --> 00:05:21.571
Now. here was a voice. one of
their own. offering them hope.

106
00:05:23.073 --> 00:05:25.909
He says. "The flame
of French resistance

107
00:05:25.909 --> 00:05:28.036
"Must not be extinguished

108
00:05:28.036 --> 00:05:30.205
and will not be extinguished."

109
00:05:30.205 --> 00:05:31.706
These are words

110
00:05:31.706 --> 00:05:34.251
that French men and women
were longing to hear.

111
00:05:35.669 --> 00:05:37.337
It's not only the beginning
of the resistance.

112
00:05:37.337 --> 00:05:39.923
it's the beginning of the
rise of Charles De Gaulle

113
00:05:39.923 --> 00:05:41.800
to political power in France.

114
00:05:44.594 --> 00:05:47.764
- Resistance pops up
all over the country.

115
00:05:47.764 --> 00:05:49.683
But these groups
aren't connected.

116
00:05:49.683 --> 00:05:51.685
and they all stand
for different things.

117
00:05:52.853 --> 00:05:54.896
- [Dan S.] There are
aristocratic Catholics.

118
00:05:54.896 --> 00:05:57.148
Communists. or Republicans.

119
00:05:57.148 --> 00:05:58.775
There are all different groups

120
00:05:58.775 --> 00:06:00.694
jockeying for position
between themselves

121
00:06:00.694 --> 00:06:02.904
and fighting against
the Germans as well.

122
00:06:04.656 --> 00:06:06.908
- [Tom] A cross-section
of French citizens.

123
00:06:06.908 --> 00:06:10.495
teachers. lawyers.
farmers. factory workers.

124
00:06:10.495 --> 00:06:12.289
take part in the resistance.

125
00:06:14.249 --> 00:06:16.585
Women play a substantial role.

126
00:06:19.629 --> 00:06:21.214
- [Robert] One of the most
important activities

127
00:06:21.214 --> 00:06:22.883
of the resistance

128
00:06:22.883 --> 00:06:26.887
was a network of agents and
secret codes and safe houses

129
00:06:26.887 --> 00:06:30.265
that helped smuggle
downed Allied flyers

130
00:06:30.265 --> 00:06:32.976
out of France and back
to safety in Britain.

131
00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:35.437
This underground railroad

132
00:06:35.437 --> 00:06:39.316
helped about 5.000 Allied
flyers make it to freedom

133
00:06:39.316 --> 00:06:40.567
in the course of the war.

134
00:06:42.402 --> 00:06:45.238
- [Col. Douds] It's the
potential of the Allied invasion

135
00:06:45.238 --> 00:06:46.656
eventually reaching France

136
00:06:46.656 --> 00:06:50.410
that cause volunteers to
flock to the resistance.

137
00:06:52.996 --> 00:06:56.583
They're gathering
equipment. supplies. weapons

138
00:06:56.583 --> 00:06:58.960
because they think:
"Eventually. we will be freed.

139
00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:00.920
How do we help
accelerate that?"

140
00:07:02.964 --> 00:07:05.884
[broadcaster speaks French]

141
00:07:05.884 --> 00:07:07.636
- [Tom] The night before D-Day.

142
00:07:07.636 --> 00:07:11.097
dozens of Allied agents are
dropped behind German lines

143
00:07:11.097 --> 00:07:14.434
to coordinate with groups
of the French resistance.

144
00:07:14.434 --> 00:07:16.895
so-called Jedburgh teams.

145
00:07:18.772 --> 00:07:19.773
- [Robert] Three-man teams

146
00:07:19.773 --> 00:07:22.484
who parachute into occupied
France:

147
00:07:22.484 --> 00:07:26.029
an American. a British.
and a French agent.

148
00:07:27.447 --> 00:07:29.115
- [Rebecca] They bring weapons
with them.

149
00:07:29.115 --> 00:07:31.576
They'd bring a radio set
so they could communicate.

150
00:07:33.203 --> 00:07:34.579
- [Robert] They coordinate
activities.

151
00:07:34.746 --> 00:07:37.749
and they inform the groups
what the Allies are up to.

152
00:07:39.084 --> 00:07:41.544
The Jedburghs have
an ultimate goal.

153
00:07:41.544 --> 00:07:42.921
and that is to ease

154
00:07:42.921 --> 00:07:46.132
the invasion plan for
June. 1944.

155
00:07:47.133 --> 00:07:49.969
[dramatic music]

156
00:07:51.388 --> 00:07:53.765
Once the Allies
land in June 6th.

157
00:07:53.765 --> 00:07:55.642
the resistance
becomes emboldened.

158
00:07:57.769 --> 00:08:00.939
- [Tom] But many pay a grievous
price for their courage.

159
00:08:00.939 --> 00:08:03.441
Germans. with the help
of French collaborators.

160
00:08:03.441 --> 00:08:06.361
hunt down anyone
assisting the Allies.

161
00:08:07.445 --> 00:08:09.155
- [Col. Douds] The costs of
being in the resistance

162
00:08:09.155 --> 00:08:11.908
or around the resistance
are incredibly high.

163
00:08:11.908 --> 00:08:13.410
They could be arrested
and put in jail.

164
00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:15.245
they could be sent
to a labor camp.

165
00:08:15.245 --> 00:08:18.415
hung oftentimes without
trial and in public

166
00:08:18.415 --> 00:08:20.083
as a deterrence
to everyone else.

167
00:08:21.876 --> 00:08:24.796
- [Robert] The Gestapo
carryout reprisals.

168
00:08:24.796 --> 00:08:26.673
"For every one of
our soldiers killed.

169
00:08:26.673 --> 00:08:28.842
we will kill 50
resistance fighters."

170
00:08:32.804 --> 00:08:35.306
- [Tom] Four days
after the invasion.

171
00:08:35.306 --> 00:08:37.267
near the village of
Oradour-sur-Glane.

172
00:08:38.268 --> 00:08:41.520
the local resistance kills
an SS battalion commander.

173
00:08:42.272 --> 00:08:46.067
Nazi retribution is
swift and merciless.

174
00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:48.778
- [Col. Douds] They take
all the men

175
00:08:48.945 --> 00:08:50.572
and they shove them into a
bunch of barns. shoot them.

176
00:08:50.572 --> 00:08:52.240
and set the barns on fire.

177
00:08:53.491 --> 00:08:54.868
They take all the
women and children

178
00:08:54.868 --> 00:08:56.161
and put them in a church

179
00:08:57.287 --> 00:08:59.122
and then burn the
church to the ground.

180
00:08:59.956 --> 00:09:02.000
shooting anyone who
tries to climb out.

181
00:09:02.834 --> 00:09:06.463
Collective vengeance for
the act of resistance.

182
00:09:10.383 --> 00:09:11.843
- [Tom] On July 25th.

183
00:09:11.843 --> 00:09:14.929
the Americans launch a
massive air and ground attack:

184
00:09:14.929 --> 00:09:16.681
Operation Cobra.

185
00:09:19.225 --> 00:09:21.186
After a month and
a half of combat

186
00:09:21.186 --> 00:09:23.563
among the hedge
rows of Normandy.

187
00:09:23.563 --> 00:09:27.942
the Allies now break through
the German defensive perimeter.

188
00:09:30.236 --> 00:09:33.656
- [Dan S.] Paris is the center
of the German occupying force.

189
00:09:33.656 --> 00:09:36.993
But the Allies were not
super interested in Paris.

190
00:09:38.244 --> 00:09:40.371
- Cities are an enormous
military problem.

191
00:09:40.371 --> 00:09:42.082
Do you go into Paris

192
00:09:42.082 --> 00:09:43.792
knowing that you could end
up fighting an urban battle

193
00:09:43.792 --> 00:09:45.752
that you don't want to fight?

194
00:09:45.752 --> 00:09:47.921
The streets narrow your
ability to maneuver.

195
00:09:47.921 --> 00:09:49.798
So fighting inside a city

196
00:09:49.798 --> 00:09:52.801
is nobody's idea of
what you want to do.

197
00:09:52.801 --> 00:09:54.010
Of course. if you're French.

198
00:09:54.010 --> 00:09:55.637
the situation is
completely different.

199
00:09:57.097 --> 00:10:00.517
- The French people are
thinking that the Allies are

200
00:10:00.517 --> 00:10:03.686
just a few hundred kilometers
from the city of Paris.

201
00:10:03.686 --> 00:10:06.481
and they believe that
Paris will be next

202
00:10:06.481 --> 00:10:09.692
in the great capitals of
Europe to be liberated

203
00:10:09.692 --> 00:10:10.985
from Nazi fascism.

204
00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:13.363
- [Michael] To the French.

205
00:10:13.363 --> 00:10:15.323
Paris is not just the
capital of France.

206
00:10:15.323 --> 00:10:18.243
It is the most important.
most symbolic city

207
00:10:18.243 --> 00:10:19.994
that you're going to liberate.

208
00:10:19.994 --> 00:10:22.330
And the man who thinks
he's going to take control

209
00:10:22.330 --> 00:10:24.124
of all of this is
Charles De Gaulle.

210
00:10:27.085 --> 00:10:29.921
- [Dan S.] Throughout the war.
he builds up Free French forces

211
00:10:29.921 --> 00:10:32.549
in London and in Africa.

212
00:10:32.549 --> 00:10:34.759
French soldiers
flock to his colors.

213
00:10:35.844 --> 00:10:37.387
And. by D-Day.

214
00:10:37.387 --> 00:10:40.682
he has something of a
Free French armed force.

215
00:10:40.682 --> 00:10:44.602
And De Gaulle is astonished
and appalled to hear

216
00:10:44.602 --> 00:10:48.231
that the Allies are
not focusing on Paris

217
00:10:48.231 --> 00:10:50.316
as a key objective.

218
00:10:50.316 --> 00:10:51.484
Paris is like the

219
00:10:51.484 --> 00:10:53.528
beating heart of the French
republic.

220
00:10:54.362 --> 00:10:56.614
There is no France
without Paris.

221
00:10:56.614 --> 00:10:59.450
And De Gaulle said. "If
you're not going. I'm going."

222
00:11:02.579 --> 00:11:05.081
[engines whir]

223
00:11:05.707 --> 00:11:08.042
- [Tom] Allied forces
advance across France

224
00:11:08.042 --> 00:11:10.920
to force the Wehrmacht's
retreat to the Rhine and beyond.

225
00:11:13.923 --> 00:11:16.384
Liberating Paris from
German occupation

226
00:11:16.384 --> 00:11:19.262
is not their main
strategic concern.

227
00:11:19.262 --> 00:11:22.765
But Parisians have other ideas.

228
00:11:22.765 --> 00:11:23.975
- [Michael] The problem for
Paris is that.

229
00:11:23.975 --> 00:11:25.602
when the Normandy invasion
happens.

230
00:11:25.602 --> 00:11:28.521
it cuts the city off from
its principal food supply.

231
00:11:29.397 --> 00:11:30.565
And the Germans
are trying to take

232
00:11:30.565 --> 00:11:32.400
everything that they
can out of the city.

233
00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:36.362
So it's a very. very
unstable. very insecure place.

234
00:11:37.614 --> 00:11:39.157
- And so there is the beginnings

235
00:11:39.157 --> 00:11:41.451
of popular uprising in Paris.

236
00:11:41.451 --> 00:11:44.996
A lot of that is being driven
by the communist resistance.

237
00:11:44.996 --> 00:11:46.414
De Gaulle is quite
keen to get to Paris

238
00:11:46.581 --> 00:11:50.126
because what he doesn't is
Paris to become liberated

239
00:11:50.126 --> 00:11:52.837
by the communist resistance.

240
00:11:52.837 --> 00:11:54.797
He does not want
to liberate France

241
00:11:54.797 --> 00:11:57.217
only for it to fall into
the hands of communism.

242
00:11:59.427 --> 00:12:02.722
- [Tom] To ensure that
French troops liberate Paris.

243
00:12:02.722 --> 00:12:04.682
General De Gaulle
requires the support

244
00:12:04.682 --> 00:12:08.728
of the Supreme Allied Commander:
General Dwight Eisenhower.

245
00:12:10.313 --> 00:12:12.190
- [Michael] De Gaulle didn't
have that many cards

246
00:12:12.190 --> 00:12:13.775
to play in 1944.

247
00:12:13.775 --> 00:12:17.111
but he played them as
skillfully as anybody.

248
00:12:17.111 --> 00:12:19.113
There is a French unit. the
Second Armored Division.

249
00:12:19.113 --> 00:12:20.907
controlled by General
Philippe Leclerc.

250
00:12:20.907 --> 00:12:22.533
that has been disobeying
American orders

251
00:12:22.533 --> 00:12:24.827
to get closer and closer
and closer to the city

252
00:12:24.827 --> 00:12:27.038
so that it could go
into Paris if it had to.

253
00:12:28.498 --> 00:12:31.209
- And so De Gaulle became
very aggressive about

254
00:12:31.209 --> 00:12:34.045
steering the Second Armored
Division toward the city.

255
00:12:34.045 --> 00:12:36.923
He gave General Leclerc
specific instructions

256
00:12:36.923 --> 00:12:38.883
to. "No matter what
these Americans tell you.

257
00:12:38.883 --> 00:12:40.843
I need you to get to Paris."

258
00:12:42.470 --> 00:12:43.721
- [Col. Douds] As Winston
Churchill would say.

259
00:12:43.721 --> 00:12:46.224
he tends to love to bite
the hand that feeds him.

260
00:12:46.224 --> 00:12:47.350
You have to give
him credit. though.

261
00:12:47.350 --> 00:12:49.435
for he has his eye on one thing.

262
00:12:49.435 --> 00:12:51.187
and that's a free France.

263
00:12:51.187 --> 00:12:53.314
[dramatic music]

264
00:12:53.314 --> 00:12:56.776
[artillery fires]

265
00:12:57.986 --> 00:12:59.529
- [Tom] Shortly after D-Day.

266
00:12:59.529 --> 00:13:02.240
in coordination with
their Western allies.

267
00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:05.118
the Soviets throw the
massed might of the Red Army

268
00:13:05.118 --> 00:13:06.369
against the Germans.

269
00:13:08.371 --> 00:13:11.416
- Operation Bagration is the
gigantic Soviet offensive

270
00:13:11.416 --> 00:13:13.001
in summer of 1944.

271
00:13:13.001 --> 00:13:14.794
Roughly timed to coincide

272
00:13:14.794 --> 00:13:16.921
with the Allies
landing in Normandy.

273
00:13:18.214 --> 00:13:21.801
- [Dan S.] It is just a
monstrous offensive.

274
00:13:21.801 --> 00:13:25.138
About two and a half million
Soviets are thrown into battle.

275
00:13:25.138 --> 00:13:28.891
Half a million Germans are
killed. wounded. or captured.

276
00:13:30.768 --> 00:13:34.522
- [Robert] It erased an entire
German army group from the map.

277
00:13:34.522 --> 00:13:38.359
the entire central portion of
the German front in the east.

278
00:13:41.946 --> 00:13:44.657
- Now the Soviet army
is moving into Poland.

279
00:13:44.657 --> 00:13:46.617
and you've gotta
remember that. of course.

280
00:13:47.243 --> 00:13:49.162
Poland was the reason
the war started.

281
00:13:51.122 --> 00:13:53.207
- [Tom] In the fall of 1939.

282
00:13:53.207 --> 00:13:56.961
Adolf Hitler and Joseph Stalin
agreed to divide Poland.

283
00:13:58.337 --> 00:13:59.964
- [Simon] When Hitler took
Poland.

284
00:13:59.964 --> 00:14:03.051
he treated it like the
laboratory

285
00:14:03.051 --> 00:14:05.011
for his racial ideas.

286
00:14:05.845 --> 00:14:09.599
his plans to annihilate
the Jewish people

287
00:14:09.599 --> 00:14:14.228
and to treat the
Eastern Slavic people

288
00:14:14.228 --> 00:14:18.441
as essentially a slave
race to work to death.

289
00:14:20.443 --> 00:14:24.322
Poland has been stained
in blood and killing.

290
00:14:24.322 --> 00:14:26.949
crushed beneath the
German jackboot.

291
00:14:26.949 --> 00:14:29.243
its soil watered with the blood

292
00:14:29.243 --> 00:14:31.829
of vast numbers of
its Jewish community.

293
00:14:33.956 --> 00:14:35.291
- [Dan S.] The Poles have been

294
00:14:35.291 --> 00:14:38.961
living through hell for five
years.

295
00:14:38.961 --> 00:14:40.588
If you're a resident of Warsaw.

296
00:14:40.588 --> 00:14:42.840
you are one of the most unlucky
people ever to have lived.

297
00:14:46.803 --> 00:14:48.679
Throughout this horror show

298
00:14:48.679 --> 00:14:50.431
that has been inflicted
upon the Poles.

299
00:14:50.431 --> 00:14:53.309
they have maintained
a shadow government.

300
00:14:53.309 --> 00:14:54.602
a kind of national cohesion.

301
00:14:54.602 --> 00:14:57.146
They have a
government-in-exile in London.

302
00:14:57.146 --> 00:14:58.481
and that is sending
orders

303
00:14:58.481 --> 00:15:01.109
to a home army. a sort of
secret army

304
00:15:01.109 --> 00:15:03.653
of underground resistance
fighters in Poland.

305
00:15:06.531 --> 00:15:08.116
- The Polish Home
Army was formed

306
00:15:08.116 --> 00:15:11.994
really immediately after
the Germans invaded.

307
00:15:11.994 --> 00:15:14.831
There was a real sense in
amongst the Polish community

308
00:15:14.831 --> 00:15:17.583
and especially the young
people: "We're gonna fight."

309
00:15:17.583 --> 00:15:20.753
It was always set up as a
formal military organization.

310
00:15:20.753 --> 00:15:23.172
It wasn't just an
ad hoc bunch of partisans.

311
00:15:24.173 --> 00:15:26.884
They undertook intelligence
gatherings of troop movements.

312
00:15:26.884 --> 00:15:28.469
They gathered information

313
00:15:28.469 --> 00:15:30.763
about the camps. about
Auschwitz. and others.

314
00:15:32.974 --> 00:15:36.269
- [Tom] The Home Army is led
by a former cavalry officer

315
00:15:36.269 --> 00:15:39.230
who has been fighting
the Germans since 1939.

316
00:15:41.607 --> 00:15:44.068
General Tadeusz Bór-Komorowski.

317
00:15:45.194 --> 00:15:47.864
[tanks rattle]

318
00:15:48.614 --> 00:15:51.367
On July 29th. 1944.

319
00:15:51.367 --> 00:15:54.162
as the Allies are
slicing through France.

320
00:15:54.162 --> 00:15:57.832
the Soviets reach the
outskirts of Warsaw.

321
00:15:57.832 --> 00:16:00.168
[guns fire]

322
00:16:00.168 --> 00:16:02.670
- [Simon] The Polish Home Army
is waiting for the moment

323
00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:04.547
when the Germans
are about to retreat

324
00:16:04.547 --> 00:16:07.550
and just before
the Soviets arrive.

325
00:16:07.550 --> 00:16:09.260
Because that is the opportunity

326
00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:13.598
to reassert Polish
independence. the Polish state.

327
00:16:16.017 --> 00:16:17.435
- [Alexandra] They wanted to
prove to the world

328
00:16:17.435 --> 00:16:19.187
that they stood
on their own feet

329
00:16:19.187 --> 00:16:22.148
and that they fought to
liberate their own country.

330
00:16:22.148 --> 00:16:24.650
They don't want to give
up the Nazi dictatorship

331
00:16:24.650 --> 00:16:27.403
and just have it replaced
with a Soviet dictatorship.

332
00:16:30.531 --> 00:16:34.202
- [Tom] The Polish Home Army
and General Bór-Komorowski

333
00:16:34.202 --> 00:16:36.120
have long planned
for this moment.

334
00:16:38.206 --> 00:16:41.876
- [Dan S.] There are weapons
caches. command structures.

335
00:16:41.876 --> 00:16:43.127
There are instructions

336
00:16:43.127 --> 00:16:45.046
coming from senior
officers based in London.

337
00:16:45.046 --> 00:16:46.088
There is a plan.

338
00:16:48.925 --> 00:16:51.427
- [Alexandra] Tens of thousands
of Home Army troops

339
00:16:51.427 --> 00:16:53.095
will go all over the city

340
00:16:53.095 --> 00:16:54.764
and go for the most
important things:

341
00:16:54.764 --> 00:16:55.973
the airports. the bridges.

342
00:16:55.973 --> 00:16:58.851
the main focal
points of Nazi power.

343
00:17:00.603 --> 00:17:03.814
- [Simon] It was a moment where
timing was everything.

344
00:17:03.814 --> 00:17:07.401
If they did it too soon. the
Germans would crush them.

345
00:17:07.401 --> 00:17:10.363
If they did it too late. the
Soviets would conquer them.

346
00:17:10.363 --> 00:17:12.906
[guns fire]

347
00:17:15.785 --> 00:17:17.203
- [Alexandra] The Red Army was
coming.

348
00:17:17.203 --> 00:17:18.746
It looked as if there
was just this juggernaut

349
00:17:18.746 --> 00:17:21.040
that was gonna force the
Germans out of the city.

350
00:17:23.291 --> 00:17:24.961
- [Tom] Outside Warsaw.

351
00:17:24.961 --> 00:17:27.880
the Germans regroup on the
banks of the Vistula River.

352
00:17:29.882 --> 00:17:31.801
- [Alexandra] The Poles hear the
sounds of fighting.

353
00:17:31.801 --> 00:17:33.094
think the Red Army's gonna win.

354
00:17:33.094 --> 00:17:35.680
and they're about
to come into Warsaw.

355
00:17:36.764 --> 00:17:38.891
- [Tom] General
Bór-Komorowski gives the order

356
00:17:38.891 --> 00:17:40.726
to begin the uprising.

357
00:17:47.567 --> 00:17:49.443
- [Tom] The Poles have
been resisting the Germans

358
00:17:49.443 --> 00:17:51.487
for almost five years.

359
00:17:51.487 --> 00:17:54.991
Now. with the Soviets on the
other side of the Vistula.

360
00:17:54.991 --> 00:17:58.494
the Polish Home Army
seizes its opportunity.

361
00:17:58.494 --> 00:18:00.705
- Because of the Red
Army coming so quickly

362
00:18:00.705 --> 00:18:02.415
up to the gates of Warsaw.

363
00:18:02.415 --> 00:18:04.083
General Bór-Komorowski
gives the order

364
00:18:04.083 --> 00:18:06.335
to start the uprising.

365
00:18:06.335 --> 00:18:08.963
- [Simon] The Polish Home Army
think this is the right moment.

366
00:18:08.963 --> 00:18:10.798
The Russians are approaching.

367
00:18:10.798 --> 00:18:13.801
the Germans are clearly
preparing to evacuate.

368
00:18:13.801 --> 00:18:16.554
and the Poles appear
out of the sewers.

369
00:18:16.554 --> 00:18:18.931
out of the backstreets.
out of the cellars.

370
00:18:18.931 --> 00:18:21.559
and they raise the
flag of rebellion.

371
00:18:23.811 --> 00:18:26.147
- [Alexandra] Civilians are
thrilled. they're jubilant.

372
00:18:26.147 --> 00:18:27.857
For the first time since 1939.

373
00:18:27.857 --> 00:18:29.650
they can sing the
national anthem.

374
00:18:29.650 --> 00:18:32.528
They push trams over.
they build barricades.

375
00:18:32.528 --> 00:18:34.280
and they make it
very. very difficult

376
00:18:34.280 --> 00:18:36.157
for the Germans to fight there.

377
00:18:37.491 --> 00:18:40.453
- [Simon] It's a heroic
moment. an exciting moment.

378
00:18:40.453 --> 00:18:44.373
Poland is rising like a
phoenix out of the ashes.

379
00:18:47.043 --> 00:18:49.920
- [Dan S.] But the attack does
not go as well as it was hoped.

380
00:18:49.920 --> 00:18:53.090
They do manage to
seize the post office.

381
00:18:53.090 --> 00:18:55.801
they manage to seize an
important German arsenal.

382
00:18:55.801 --> 00:18:57.928
but they don't manage
to secure the airport.

383
00:18:57.928 --> 00:19:00.681
So it's up for grabs
by the end of that day.

384
00:19:00.681 --> 00:19:03.643
You get this sort
of twisted patchwork

385
00:19:03.643 --> 00:19:05.603
of Polish and German control.

386
00:19:05.603 --> 00:19:08.147
which ensures that
there is gonna be

387
00:19:08.147 --> 00:19:10.232
the mother of all street fights.

388
00:19:10.524 --> 00:19:12.151
[guns fire]

389
00:19:12.151 --> 00:19:13.402
- [Tom] The Polish Home Army

390
00:19:13.402 --> 00:19:15.529
expected the Germans
controlling Warsaw

391
00:19:15.529 --> 00:19:17.073
to be in retreat.

392
00:19:19.367 --> 00:19:21.243
[gun fires]

393
00:19:21.243 --> 00:19:24.872
Instead. the Wehrmacht attacks
the Red Army at the Vistula

394
00:19:25.831 --> 00:19:27.708
and retains their
grip on Warsaw.

395
00:19:32.088 --> 00:19:34.215
Adolf Hitler meets
with Heinrich Himmler.

396
00:19:34.215 --> 00:19:35.925
head of the Nazi SS.

397
00:19:38.094 --> 00:19:40.429
- [Alexandra] On the night of
the 1st of August. 1944.

398
00:19:40.429 --> 00:19:43.349
Himmler goes up to the
Wolfsschanze. he sees Hitler.

399
00:19:43.349 --> 00:19:45.685
and Hitler's raging around.

400
00:19:45.685 --> 00:19:47.812
And Himmler goes. "You
know. Mein Fuhrer.

401
00:19:47.812 --> 00:19:50.231
let's use this as a moment

402
00:19:50.231 --> 00:19:52.608
to erase Warsaw from the map.

403
00:19:52.608 --> 00:19:55.152
Because this wretched
city has stood in our way

404
00:19:55.152 --> 00:19:57.613
of German expansion to the
East for hundreds of years.

405
00:19:57.613 --> 00:19:59.281
So let's just do it."

406
00:20:00.241 --> 00:20:01.450
So that evening.

407
00:20:01.617 --> 00:20:03.369
they give what's called
the Order for Warsaw.

408
00:20:05.454 --> 00:20:06.997
- The Germans have a plan

409
00:20:06.997 --> 00:20:10.042
to wipe Poland literally
from the face of the Earth.

410
00:20:11.293 --> 00:20:13.754
One. you wanna send a
message to everybody else

411
00:20:13.754 --> 00:20:15.965
that this is the
cost of defying me.

412
00:20:15.965 --> 00:20:18.008
But the other reason is
you want to destroy Poland

413
00:20:18.008 --> 00:20:20.302
as a national identity.

414
00:20:21.971 --> 00:20:24.682
- [Alexandra] Every combatant
is to be killed.

415
00:20:24.682 --> 00:20:27.977
All men and women and children.
civilians are to be killed.

416
00:20:27.977 --> 00:20:29.645
And then the city
is to be looted.

417
00:20:29.645 --> 00:20:33.399
and then. glattrasiert. that
means razed to the ground.

418
00:20:33.399 --> 00:20:36.110
[missiles fire]

419
00:20:37.695 --> 00:20:40.364
- [Simon] The Germans poured
in ruthless shock troops

420
00:20:40.364 --> 00:20:43.117
with the express purpose
of destroying them.

421
00:20:43.117 --> 00:20:47.037
The Home Army used up their
caches of weapons very fast.

422
00:20:47.037 --> 00:20:49.165
[artillery fires]

423
00:20:49.165 --> 00:20:51.959
- [Anand] The Poles think
the Red Army is coming.

424
00:20:51.959 --> 00:20:53.919
The whole thing
depended upon the idea

425
00:20:53.919 --> 00:20:55.755
that Germans were either leaving

426
00:20:55.755 --> 00:20:59.383
or could not reinforce their
existing troops within Warsaw.

427
00:21:00.843 --> 00:21:02.970
Neither of those two things
turned out to be true.

428
00:21:02.970 --> 00:21:06.056
The Germans weren't leaving
and the Soviets weren't coming.

429
00:21:07.641 --> 00:21:09.268
- [Simon] Stalin says. to
everyone's surprise:

430
00:21:09.268 --> 00:21:11.228
"Halt the army."

431
00:21:12.396 --> 00:21:15.441
They can see across the
river Warsaw is burning.

432
00:21:18.110 --> 00:21:20.446
- [Robert] Although the Soviets
are right across the river.

433
00:21:20.446 --> 00:21:24.158
they make no effort to
support the resistance

434
00:21:24.158 --> 00:21:25.993
in its uprising
against the Germans.

435
00:21:25.993 --> 00:21:27.578
Rather. they sit
across the river

436
00:21:27.578 --> 00:21:30.080
and watch the Germans
crush the uprising.

437
00:21:32.041 --> 00:21:33.209
- [Dan S.] Stalin doesn't
just do nothing.

438
00:21:33.375 --> 00:21:35.586
He actively interferes
with the Allies' attempt

439
00:21:35.586 --> 00:21:37.755
to help the Home Army.
the Polish uprising.

440
00:21:39.799 --> 00:21:41.467
- [Tom] From their
air bases in Italy.

441
00:21:41.467 --> 00:21:45.429
the British and Americans try
to drop supplies to the Poles.

442
00:21:46.388 --> 00:21:47.723
- [Dan S.] Churchill asks
permission:

443
00:21:47.723 --> 00:21:49.850
"Can Allied planes
carrying supplies

444
00:21:49.850 --> 00:21:53.521
use Soviet bases to refuel
and carry out repairs?"

445
00:21:53.521 --> 00:21:54.647
Stalin says no.

446
00:21:56.357 --> 00:21:57.983
- [Simon] Stalin has always
hated

447
00:21:57.983 --> 00:21:59.527
the Polish government in London.

448
00:21:59.527 --> 00:22:02.696
which he regards as bourgeois.
imperialist. capitalist.

449
00:22:02.696 --> 00:22:07.117
He has a small Polish
communist group.

450
00:22:07.117 --> 00:22:08.410
He is always planning

451
00:22:08.619 --> 00:22:11.914
to parachute them into
Poland as his government.

452
00:22:11.914 --> 00:22:15.084
- [Alexandra] And so Stalin is
content to watch the Germans

453
00:22:15.084 --> 00:22:17.086
utterly destroy the
Polish Home Army.

454
00:22:17.086 --> 00:22:19.255
destroy the
population of Warsaw.

455
00:22:19.255 --> 00:22:21.465
and destroy the city itself.

456
00:22:21.465 --> 00:22:23.801
- [Simon] Stalin wants to
conquer Poland.

457
00:22:23.801 --> 00:22:25.386
but he wants to make sure

458
00:22:25.386 --> 00:22:29.348
that it's then turned into a
communist vassal client state.

459
00:22:29.348 --> 00:22:30.933
He wants to wait

460
00:22:30.933 --> 00:22:34.562
until the Nazis have destroyed
the Polish underground

461
00:22:34.562 --> 00:22:37.147
before he enters Warsaw.

462
00:22:37.147 --> 00:22:39.400
Let them do his work for him.

463
00:22:42.027 --> 00:22:46.782
Hitler sends in his
most psychopathic crews

464
00:22:46.782 --> 00:22:50.536
of murderous. grotesque sadists.

465
00:22:52.454 --> 00:22:54.915
- [Alexandra] Some of them are
the most brutal murderers

466
00:22:54.915 --> 00:22:56.709
of the entire Nazi regime.

467
00:22:56.709 --> 00:22:58.043
Like Oskar Dirlewanger.

468
00:22:58.043 --> 00:23:00.004
Even the SS doesn't
wanna work with him.

469
00:23:01.297 --> 00:23:03.591
They go through
building after building.

470
00:23:03.591 --> 00:23:05.801
then they round these
people up by the thousands

471
00:23:05.801 --> 00:23:07.511
and then mow those people down.

472
00:23:09.555 --> 00:23:11.849
When 5.000. 6.000
bodies are lying there.

473
00:23:11.849 --> 00:23:14.268
then they bring in the
verbrennungskommando.

474
00:23:14.268 --> 00:23:17.062
these burning commandos.
to burn the bodies.

475
00:23:17.062 --> 00:23:18.856
specialists from
places like Treblinka

476
00:23:18.856 --> 00:23:22.109
who've now become specialists
at burning human flesh.

477
00:23:24.445 --> 00:23:27.573
They loot the entire city
and then they destroy it.

478
00:23:28.949 --> 00:23:32.077
Building by building.
libraries. museums.

479
00:23:32.077 --> 00:23:33.162
everything is destroyed.

480
00:23:33.871 --> 00:23:36.707
[dramatic music]

481
00:23:37.958 --> 00:23:39.877
- [Tom] The Polish
Home Army surrenders.

482
00:23:41.670 --> 00:23:45.466
General Bór-Komorowski is
imprisoned by the Nazis.

483
00:23:48.677 --> 00:23:49.845
- [Alexandra] By the end of the
Warsaw Uprising.

484
00:23:50.012 --> 00:23:52.014
about 200.000 civilians
have been killed.

485
00:23:54.266 --> 00:23:56.226
Then the remaining population

486
00:23:56.226 --> 00:23:57.895
are sent to camps
like Auschwitz.

487
00:23:58.687 --> 00:24:01.482
Some are sent as slave
laborers into the Reich.

488
00:24:01.482 --> 00:24:02.775
So the destruction of Warsaw

489
00:24:02.775 --> 00:24:05.069
is unique in the
destruction of cities

490
00:24:05.069 --> 00:24:06.695
in the Second World
War in Europe.

491
00:24:08.155 --> 00:24:09.531
- [Dan S.] In Warsaw.
tragically.

492
00:24:09.531 --> 00:24:13.035
the Germans chose
to meet that desire

493
00:24:13.035 --> 00:24:16.288
for Polish independence
with overwhelming force.

494
00:24:18.916 --> 00:24:20.209
To kill and smash

495
00:24:20.209 --> 00:24:22.586
and eradicate Polish culture.

496
00:24:25.339 --> 00:24:27.800
To say. "No. you will
not liberate yourselves.

497
00:24:27.800 --> 00:24:30.052
In fact. you have
condemned yourselves."

498
00:24:30.552 --> 00:24:32.471
[soft plaintive music]

499
00:24:35.599 --> 00:24:38.435
[dramatic music]

500
00:24:38.644 --> 00:24:40.896
- [Tom] August. 1944.

501
00:24:40.896 --> 00:24:44.024
The Germans are in full
retreat across France.

502
00:24:45.693 --> 00:24:47.903
They begin to evacuate Paris.

503
00:24:49.238 --> 00:24:52.491
Parisians sense a chance
to recapture their city.

504
00:24:53.993 --> 00:24:55.995
But they need a spark.

505
00:24:55.995 --> 00:24:57.287
- [Marco] On the 15th of August.

506
00:24:57.454 --> 00:25:00.082
the Parisian police.
which was 20.000 strong

507
00:25:00.082 --> 00:25:02.001
and had been collaborating
with the Germans

508
00:25:02.001 --> 00:25:03.502
up to that moment.

509
00:25:03.502 --> 00:25:06.922
actually switches sides
and raises the flag

510
00:25:06.922 --> 00:25:07.965
on their building.

511
00:25:09.133 --> 00:25:11.802
giving the sign to
the rest of the city

512
00:25:11.802 --> 00:25:13.470
and of course to the partisans

513
00:25:13.470 --> 00:25:17.016
that this was the moment the
uprising needed to start.

514
00:25:17.016 --> 00:25:19.977
[guns fire]

515
00:25:19.977 --> 00:25:23.230
And so the streets of
Paris become a battlefield.

516
00:25:26.358 --> 00:25:27.776
[guns fire]

517
00:25:27.985 --> 00:25:30.446
The French resistance has
been preparing for this moment

518
00:25:30.446 --> 00:25:32.406
for a very long time.

519
00:25:32.406 --> 00:25:35.617
[dramatic music]

520
00:25:35.617 --> 00:25:38.037
And they act against the
Germans quite rapidly

521
00:25:38.037 --> 00:25:39.163
and quite successfully.

522
00:25:42.791 --> 00:25:44.752
- [Col. Douds] As this happened.
Charles De Gaulle shows up

523
00:25:44.752 --> 00:25:47.171
to Supreme Allied Commander
Eisenhower's headquarters

524
00:25:47.171 --> 00:25:49.131
and go. "We have
to march on Paris."

525
00:25:50.883 --> 00:25:52.551
- [Michael] The Allies don't
particularly like De Gaulle

526
00:25:52.551 --> 00:25:54.887
but see De Gaulle as a
bulwark against communism.

527
00:25:55.929 --> 00:25:57.264
- [Dan S.] Eisenhower is
smart enough to know

528
00:25:57.264 --> 00:25:58.766
that it would look
really. really good

529
00:25:58.766 --> 00:26:01.685
if the first troops to enter
Paris were the Free French.

530
00:26:03.562 --> 00:26:06.732
- [Marco] Eisenhower decides
to allow the Free French forces

531
00:26:06.732 --> 00:26:08.859
to send one division
into the city

532
00:26:08.859 --> 00:26:12.029
and to help the insurgents
to free the city.

533
00:26:13.906 --> 00:26:16.533
- [Tom] Supported by the
American Fourth Division.

534
00:26:16.533 --> 00:26:18.202
General De Gaulle orders

535
00:26:18.202 --> 00:26:21.413
the French Second Armored
Division into Paris.

536
00:26:23.165 --> 00:26:24.666
- [Martin] Toward the bitter
end.

537
00:26:24.792 --> 00:26:27.211
Adolf Hitler issues instructions
to General von Choltitz.

538
00:26:27.211 --> 00:26:30.464
who was in control of
Paris. to destroy the city.

539
00:26:30.464 --> 00:26:32.549
Hitler. during a phone
call with von Choltitz.

540
00:26:32.549 --> 00:26:34.551
asks: "Is Paris burning?"

541
00:26:35.803 --> 00:26:37.346
And von Choltitz tells him:

542
00:26:37.346 --> 00:26:39.973
"Yes. my Fuhrer. we
burned the city."

543
00:26:39.973 --> 00:26:42.684
[dramatic music]

544
00:26:44.353 --> 00:26:46.855
- [Tom] Instead. he
negotiates a surrender.

545
00:26:49.108 --> 00:26:52.027
[triumphant music]

546
00:26:56.907 --> 00:26:58.200
- [Dan S.] It is the officers
and men

547
00:26:58.200 --> 00:27:00.828
of that Second French
Armored Division

548
00:27:00.828 --> 00:27:02.830
who first enter Paris.

549
00:27:02.830 --> 00:27:05.707
[triumphant music]

550
00:27:07.876 --> 00:27:09.378
So De Gaulle can claim

551
00:27:09.378 --> 00:27:11.839
that the French have
liberated their own capital.

552
00:27:11.839 --> 00:27:12.965
And not just any old French.

553
00:27:12.965 --> 00:27:14.716
not the communist
French. not the resis...

554
00:27:14.716 --> 00:27:16.885
His French army.

555
00:27:16.885 --> 00:27:19.805
[triumphant music]

556
00:27:22.015 --> 00:27:24.226
- [Marco] He is the one
leading the parade

557
00:27:24.226 --> 00:27:26.645
through the streets of Paris.
presenting himself

558
00:27:26.645 --> 00:27:28.188
as the legitimate leader

559
00:27:28.188 --> 00:27:29.523
of the French government.

560
00:27:29.523 --> 00:27:32.484
[triumphant music]

561
00:27:34.611 --> 00:27:36.572
- [Martin] Everyone in the
city has come out now

562
00:27:36.572 --> 00:27:37.906
because they feel

563
00:27:37.906 --> 00:27:39.408
that their moment of
liberation is on them.

564
00:27:42.286 --> 00:27:47.332
[guns fire]
[citizens scream]

565
00:27:49.751 --> 00:27:52.921
- [Tom] But there are still
German soldiers in Paris.

566
00:27:55.757 --> 00:28:00.762
[guns fire]
[foreboding music]

567
00:28:04.266 --> 00:28:05.851
- [Broadcaster] General De
Gaulle walked straight ahead

568
00:28:05.851 --> 00:28:08.604
into what appeared to
me to be a hail of fire.

569
00:28:08.604 --> 00:28:11.356
But he went straight
ahead without hesitation.

570
00:28:11.356 --> 00:28:12.691
his shoulders flung back.

571
00:28:13.942 --> 00:28:16.361
[guns fire]

572
00:28:17.613 --> 00:28:20.198
- [Tom] Most Germans
have surrendered or fled.

573
00:28:20.198 --> 00:28:22.200
The last are tracked down.

574
00:28:22.200 --> 00:28:23.994
along with French collaborators.

575
00:28:26.038 --> 00:28:28.749
[crowd cheers]

576
00:28:31.919 --> 00:28:36.214
[Charles speaks French]

577
00:28:41.595 --> 00:28:44.723
[Charles speaks French]

578
00:28:48.226 --> 00:28:50.520
- [Marco] He presents the
liberation of Paris

579
00:28:50.520 --> 00:28:52.648
as a story of self-liberation.

580
00:28:52.648 --> 00:28:54.650
a Paris that was
able to free itself

581
00:28:54.650 --> 00:28:57.152
from the yoke of the
German occupation

582
00:28:57.152 --> 00:28:59.112
without the help of the Allies.

583
00:28:59.947 --> 00:29:03.241
[Charles speaks French]

584
00:29:05.619 --> 00:29:08.914
- [Marco] However. let's say the
Allied presence in the region

585
00:29:08.914 --> 00:29:10.290
also played a major role

586
00:29:10.499 --> 00:29:12.834
in convincing the Germans
that it was time to go.

587
00:29:14.836 --> 00:29:16.755
- [Michael] France will enter
this post-war period

588
00:29:16.755 --> 00:29:18.840
with pride and with confidence.

589
00:29:18.840 --> 00:29:20.968
It's difficult to imagine
how that would've happened

590
00:29:20.968 --> 00:29:23.762
if not for the efforts of
all of the resistance groups

591
00:29:23.762 --> 00:29:25.889
that fought against
Nazi aggression.

592
00:29:25.889 --> 00:29:28.725
[crowd applauds]

593
00:29:33.730 --> 00:29:36.942
- [Tom] But. in Warsaw.
the outcome is different.

594
00:29:36.942 --> 00:29:39.361
The city is razed by the Nazis.

595
00:29:41.613 --> 00:29:45.784
And what remains is captured
and controlled by the Soviets.

596
00:29:45.784 --> 00:29:48.453
[dramatic music]

597
00:29:49.579 --> 00:29:52.290
Paris. the city of
light. is saved.

598
00:29:54.251 --> 00:29:58.463
Warsaw and the hope of the
Polish people is destroyed.

599
00:30:01.591 --> 00:30:04.761
[soft plaintive music]

600
00:30:10.684 --> 00:30:12.894
- [Alexandra] Resistance is
important

601
00:30:12.894 --> 00:30:15.355
for the people of a nation

602
00:30:15.355 --> 00:30:17.274
who do not want
to be subjugated.

603
00:30:19.192 --> 00:30:23.071
While the French and the Poles
rise up against the Germans

604
00:30:23.071 --> 00:30:26.241
within the extermination
camps at Auschwitz and others.

605
00:30:27.409 --> 00:30:30.037
there is also this
spirit of resistance.

606
00:30:31.079 --> 00:30:34.249
[soft plaintive music]

607
00:30:35.709 --> 00:30:38.045
- [Tom] The deliberate
killing of Jews by the Nazis

608
00:30:38.045 --> 00:30:39.796
began with bullets.

609
00:30:42.924 --> 00:30:44.384
Shortly after the Germans

610
00:30:44.384 --> 00:30:47.721
invaded the Soviet
Union in June. 1941.

611
00:30:49.598 --> 00:30:52.642
they started to shoot Jewish
children. women. and men.

612
00:30:53.810 --> 00:30:56.521
and bury them in
ravines and forests.

613
00:31:00.233 --> 00:31:01.568
But eventually the Nazis

614
00:31:01.568 --> 00:31:05.363
required a more systematic
method of murder.

615
00:31:06.990 --> 00:31:08.617
- There were tens of
thousands of the camps

616
00:31:08.617 --> 00:31:09.451
by the end of the war.

617
00:31:09.451 --> 00:31:11.119
It's a huge infrastructure.

618
00:31:11.119 --> 00:31:14.664
But there were a very
small number of death camps

619
00:31:14.664 --> 00:31:17.000
within this enormous system.

620
00:31:17.000 --> 00:31:19.669
Six death camps.

621
00:31:19.669 --> 00:31:21.630
Chelmno. which is the
original death camp.

622
00:31:21.630 --> 00:31:23.548
Belzec. Sobibor. Treblinka.

623
00:31:23.548 --> 00:31:26.635
and then Auschwitz-Birkenau
and Majdanek.

624
00:31:29.012 --> 00:31:31.765
- [Tom] As the Red Army
pushes into Poland.

625
00:31:31.765 --> 00:31:34.434
the Nazis attempt to
hide their great crime

626
00:31:34.434 --> 00:31:36.812
by dismantling the
killing centers.

627
00:31:40.482 --> 00:31:42.025
- [James] They destroyed
documents.

628
00:31:42.025 --> 00:31:43.610
They destroyed the
major death camps

629
00:31:43.610 --> 00:31:46.113
at Belzec. Sobibor.
and Treblinka.

630
00:31:46.113 --> 00:31:48.198
They get rid of the vast
majority of the buildings.

631
00:31:48.198 --> 00:31:49.241
They cover them over.

632
00:31:49.241 --> 00:31:51.243
They create
farmhouses on the site

633
00:31:51.243 --> 00:31:52.577
to conceal everything
that they've done.

634
00:31:52.577 --> 00:31:55.413
And then as the camps
come into the path

635
00:31:55.413 --> 00:31:59.126
of invading Allied armies.
they evacuate them.

636
00:31:59.126 --> 00:32:02.003
[dramatic music]

637
00:32:03.547 --> 00:32:05.674
- [Tom] At Auschwitz-Birkenau.
there are prisoners

638
00:32:05.674 --> 00:32:09.386
determined to document
the Nazi murders.

639
00:32:09.386 --> 00:32:11.596
- [Alexandra] The extermination
camp system

640
00:32:11.596 --> 00:32:13.974
includes the sorting of people.

641
00:32:13.974 --> 00:32:16.518
Some of them are left
alive because they looked

642
00:32:16.518 --> 00:32:19.187
as if they were young or
fit or healthy enough.

643
00:32:19.187 --> 00:32:22.399
Those people work in something
called a sonderkommando.

644
00:32:22.399 --> 00:32:25.402
They are supposed to
sort the clothing.

645
00:32:25.402 --> 00:32:27.445
sort the hair that's
been cut off people.

646
00:32:27.445 --> 00:32:29.281
to move the corpses.

647
00:32:29.281 --> 00:32:31.867
all these terrible. terrible
jobs that they're given.

648
00:32:33.410 --> 00:32:35.787
- [James] In 1944 in
Auschwitz-Birkenau.

649
00:32:35.787 --> 00:32:37.038
members of the
resistance movement

650
00:32:37.038 --> 00:32:39.875
managed to smuggle a
camera into the camp.

651
00:32:39.875 --> 00:32:41.168
The sonderkommando.

652
00:32:41.168 --> 00:32:42.294
who were witnessing
all of these things

653
00:32:42.294 --> 00:32:43.170
on a day-to-day basis.

654
00:32:43.170 --> 00:32:44.796
secure this camera.

655
00:32:44.796 --> 00:32:46.923
and their ambition is to
use this camera to record

656
00:32:46.923 --> 00:32:49.926
images of what's
happening within the camp

657
00:32:49.926 --> 00:32:53.972
in order for those pictures
to stir the world to respond.

658
00:32:55.473 --> 00:32:58.518
- [Tom] One prisoner manages
to photograph the horror.

659
00:33:00.520 --> 00:33:03.190
[clock ticks]

660
00:33:03.982 --> 00:33:06.610
[foreboding music]

661
00:33:06.610 --> 00:33:09.196
Four images of hell.

662
00:33:10.197 --> 00:33:12.073
- [James] The really
extraordinary thing about them

663
00:33:12.073 --> 00:33:14.868
is that the images
themselves are framed

664
00:33:14.868 --> 00:33:18.914
from within the doorway of the
gas chamber building itself.

665
00:33:18.914 --> 00:33:22.709
So the images testify
to the bravery

666
00:33:22.709 --> 00:33:24.294
of what this man was doing

667
00:33:24.294 --> 00:33:28.924
and the appalling depravity
of the process at that stage.

668
00:33:30.258 --> 00:33:32.385
And what we see
within these images

669
00:33:32.385 --> 00:33:35.639
are the burning of bodies
outside the crematoria.

670
00:33:37.724 --> 00:33:39.851
And we see people
being undressed.

671
00:33:39.851 --> 00:33:42.562
waiting to be brought
into the gas chamber.

672
00:33:42.562 --> 00:33:45.899
[foreboding music]

673
00:33:45.899 --> 00:33:48.610
- [Alexandra] The film is put
into a tube of toothpaste

674
00:33:48.610 --> 00:33:51.321
and then smuggled out
to the Polish Home Army.

675
00:33:53.114 --> 00:33:56.326
This is perhaps the
single most valuable

676
00:33:56.326 --> 00:34:00.288
photographic evidence to
emerge from Auschwitz.

677
00:34:00.288 --> 00:34:03.375
[foreboding music]

678
00:34:03.375 --> 00:34:07.796
- The Jews fought back from
1933 to the end of the war.

679
00:34:07.796 --> 00:34:09.297
There are escapes.

680
00:34:09.464 --> 00:34:13.217
There are individual killings
of guards by prisoners.

681
00:34:13.217 --> 00:34:16.304
The Jews are fighting
back this entire time.

682
00:34:17.389 --> 00:34:19.975
Resistance is what
gives us testimony

683
00:34:19.975 --> 00:34:21.935
of what happened
in those places.

684
00:34:21.935 --> 00:34:24.728
[dramatic music]

685
00:34:26.356 --> 00:34:28.358
- [Tom] In the fall of 1944.

686
00:34:28.358 --> 00:34:33.237
Nazi Germany is retreating
on all fronts except one:

687
00:34:33.237 --> 00:34:35.282
their war against the Jews.

688
00:34:36.658 --> 00:34:39.244
Auschwitz-Birkenau
is now the frontline.

689
00:34:43.956 --> 00:34:46.001
- [Rebecca] Killing Jews is not
a secondary byproduct

690
00:34:46.001 --> 00:34:47.043
of their military goals.

691
00:34:47.043 --> 00:34:49.004
It is their military goal.

692
00:34:51.922 --> 00:34:53.049
- [James] From Hitler's
perspective.

693
00:34:53.257 --> 00:34:55.635
it's worth apportioning
resources to it

694
00:34:55.635 --> 00:34:58.096
despite what's
happening elsewhere.

695
00:34:58.096 --> 00:35:01.016
In fact. they accelerate
the process of mass murder.

696
00:35:04.185 --> 00:35:07.480
- [Alexandra] In 1944. the Nazis
decide to exterminate

697
00:35:07.480 --> 00:35:10.108
the Hungarian Jewish population.

698
00:35:10.108 --> 00:35:13.361
So a special train line is
built into Auschwitz-Birkenau.

699
00:35:13.361 --> 00:35:17.157
and hundreds of thousands of
Hungarian Jews are rounded up.

700
00:35:17.157 --> 00:35:18.575
and many of them are murdered

701
00:35:18.700 --> 00:35:21.745
within a few hours of
their arriving at the camp.

702
00:35:23.872 --> 00:35:28.001
- [James] 1944 is when Auschwitz
hits its

703
00:35:28.001 --> 00:35:30.045
terrible. appalling peak

704
00:35:30.045 --> 00:35:32.881
in terms of the number
of people killed.

705
00:35:34.007 --> 00:35:37.010
[foreboding music]

706
00:35:38.428 --> 00:35:40.889
- [Rebecca] Requests start to
come to the U.S. government

707
00:35:40.889 --> 00:35:43.058
to bomb either the rail lines.

708
00:35:43.058 --> 00:35:46.269
the gas chambers at
Auschwitz. the crematoria.

709
00:35:46.269 --> 00:35:49.105
the bridges leading
to these camps.

710
00:35:51.316 --> 00:35:52.734
- [Michael] The idea of
bombing death camps

711
00:35:52.901 --> 00:35:55.362
is difficult operationally
and it's difficult morally.

712
00:35:56.321 --> 00:35:57.656
You're going to
kill the very people

713
00:35:57.656 --> 00:35:59.240
that you're trying to rescue.

714
00:36:02.035 --> 00:36:03.953
- [James] Churchill and
Roosevelt's perspective:

715
00:36:03.953 --> 00:36:05.246
humanitarian concerns

716
00:36:05.246 --> 00:36:07.290
were always secondary
to winning the war.

717
00:36:07.290 --> 00:36:08.249
They would say:

718
00:36:08.249 --> 00:36:10.043
"The best thing that we can do

719
00:36:10.043 --> 00:36:11.586
to help these people

720
00:36:11.586 --> 00:36:14.005
is to bring the war to an
end as quickly as possible."

721
00:36:17.133 --> 00:36:19.052
- [Tom] Prisoners at
Auschwitz-Birkenau

722
00:36:19.052 --> 00:36:22.097
cannot afford to wait
for the war to end.

723
00:36:22.097 --> 00:36:25.850
They want to survive.
they want to escape.

724
00:36:25.850 --> 00:36:27.852
and they want to
stop the killing.

725
00:36:32.524 --> 00:36:35.694
[foreboding music]

726
00:36:35.694 --> 00:36:37.070
- [Tom] From the beginning.

727
00:36:37.070 --> 00:36:39.447
there has been an
underground of resistance

728
00:36:39.447 --> 00:36:41.241
at Auschwitz-Birkenau

729
00:36:41.241 --> 00:36:43.243
made up of various
groups of prisoners.

730
00:36:43.243 --> 00:36:45.161
including the Sonderkommando.

731
00:36:46.788 --> 00:36:48.665
- The Sonderkommando
starts to realize

732
00:36:48.665 --> 00:36:50.125
that there are fewer and fewer

733
00:36:50.125 --> 00:36:52.627
of these transports
coming from Hungary.

734
00:36:52.627 --> 00:36:56.589
"The extermination process is
finishing. our time is coming.

735
00:36:56.589 --> 00:36:59.718
and if we don't act quickly.
we're going to be killed."

736
00:37:00.760 --> 00:37:03.763
- [James] So they started to
look at ways they could resist.

737
00:37:03.763 --> 00:37:05.557
They might die trying.

738
00:37:05.557 --> 00:37:07.600
there's a very good chance
that they'll die trying.

739
00:37:07.600 --> 00:37:10.478
but at least they can
give themselves a chance.

740
00:37:10.478 --> 00:37:13.565
Because they're determined
to do whatever they can

741
00:37:13.565 --> 00:37:16.109
to meet the moment
on their own terms.

742
00:37:17.318 --> 00:37:19.946
- [Rebecca] Auschwitz is a
death camp.

743
00:37:19.946 --> 00:37:22.615
and it's also a
forced labor facility.

744
00:37:22.615 --> 00:37:24.284
And so there were women

745
00:37:24.284 --> 00:37:26.369
who were working in
the armaments factories

746
00:37:26.369 --> 00:37:27.996
nearby Auschwitz

747
00:37:27.996 --> 00:37:30.331
who had access to gunpowder.

748
00:37:32.000 --> 00:37:33.168
- [Tom] One member
of the underground

749
00:37:33.168 --> 00:37:34.878
is part of a slave labor unit

750
00:37:34.878 --> 00:37:38.840
that sorts the clothing
of the dead: Roza Robota.

751
00:37:38.840 --> 00:37:41.384
She makes contact with
the Sonderkommando.

752
00:37:41.384 --> 00:37:44.554
They form a plan to
destroy the crematoria.

753
00:37:44.554 --> 00:37:48.683
provoke a wider uprising.
and attempt to escape.

754
00:37:50.268 --> 00:37:53.772
Robota enlists Jewish women
working in the munitions factory

755
00:37:53.772 --> 00:37:55.565
to smuggle gunpowder:

756
00:37:56.483 --> 00:38:01.279
Ala Gertner. Regina Safirsztajn.
and Esther Wajcblum.

757
00:38:01.279 --> 00:38:04.073
- [James] So they secured these
explosives at enormous risk.

758
00:38:04.073 --> 00:38:05.200
and they're smuggling
them through

759
00:38:05.200 --> 00:38:06.826
various kind of ingenious means.

760
00:38:06.826 --> 00:38:09.788
They create little packets of
fabric that they hide them in.

761
00:38:09.788 --> 00:38:11.247
and they get them through.

762
00:38:13.500 --> 00:38:15.376
- [Rebecca] The women are
collecting the gunpowder

763
00:38:15.376 --> 00:38:17.086
to hand them off to
their friend Roza.

764
00:38:17.086 --> 00:38:18.797
She is going to
get the gunpowder

765
00:38:18.797 --> 00:38:20.673
into the hands of
the Sonderkommando.

766
00:38:20.673 --> 00:38:22.884
who are going to use
it to try to blow up

767
00:38:22.884 --> 00:38:24.761
the crematoria where
they're working.

768
00:38:27.680 --> 00:38:29.057
- [James] At a certain
moment.

769
00:38:29.057 --> 00:38:30.725
an individual takes
an improvised weapon

770
00:38:30.725 --> 00:38:33.228
and launches at a
member of the SS.

771
00:38:33.228 --> 00:38:35.730
and that kickstarts
the whole thing.

772
00:38:35.730 --> 00:38:38.149
[guns fire]

773
00:38:38.149 --> 00:38:40.318
There's smoke. there's
noise. there's shouting.

774
00:38:40.318 --> 00:38:41.486
and there's gunfire.

775
00:38:42.946 --> 00:38:45.573
[bomb explodes]

776
00:38:45.573 --> 00:38:49.202
- [Alexandra] The resistors
end up blowing up. in part.

777
00:38:49.202 --> 00:38:51.120
one of the crematoria.
Crematoria IV.

778
00:38:53.122 --> 00:38:54.874
- [James] It's put
permanently out of commission.

779
00:38:54.874 --> 00:38:56.125
So the Sonderkommando

780
00:38:56.125 --> 00:38:58.169
would've considered
that to be a a success.

781
00:38:59.629 --> 00:39:00.964
And in another
crematory building.

782
00:39:00.964 --> 00:39:03.842
the Sonderkommando
force some the guards

783
00:39:03.842 --> 00:39:05.760
into one of the ovens alive.

784
00:39:09.806 --> 00:39:11.599
- [Tom] Some prisoners escape

785
00:39:11.599 --> 00:39:13.434
but are quickly
caught and executed.

786
00:39:17.438 --> 00:39:19.482
- [Alexandra] The three women
who have been

787
00:39:19.482 --> 00:39:20.984
stealing the gunpowder

788
00:39:20.984 --> 00:39:23.987
plus Roza Robota
are to be hanged.

789
00:39:25.238 --> 00:39:28.199
And just as the noose
is put around her neck.

790
00:39:28.199 --> 00:39:32.161
she yells out to the group
of prisoners assembled:

791
00:39:34.038 --> 00:39:37.000
"Vengeance." or.
"Sisters. avenge."

792
00:39:38.167 --> 00:39:40.003
It is a passionate message

793
00:39:40.003 --> 00:39:43.423
of resilience. resistance.
and continue the fight.

794
00:39:46.885 --> 00:39:48.303
- [Tom] In December.

795
00:39:48.303 --> 00:39:50.722
the Red Army advances
on Auschwitz-Birkenau.

796
00:39:51.764 --> 00:39:55.393
[foreboding music]

797
00:39:55.393 --> 00:39:58.646
The SS forced prisoners
on marches into Germany.

798
00:40:02.233 --> 00:40:03.192
Thousands die.

799
00:40:04.319 --> 00:40:07.655
[foreboding music]

800
00:40:07.655 --> 00:40:11.200
On January 27th. 1945.

801
00:40:11.200 --> 00:40:15.121
Soviet soldiers arrive at the
gates of Auschwitz-Birkenau.

802
00:40:17.832 --> 00:40:20.460
- [James] Auschwitz really
became the center

803
00:40:20.460 --> 00:40:23.713
of the mass murder of Europe's
Jews quite late in the war.

804
00:40:23.713 --> 00:40:25.048
The important thing to know

805
00:40:25.256 --> 00:40:29.344
is that 80% of the Jews
murdered in the Holocaust

806
00:40:29.344 --> 00:40:30.762
were not murdered at Auschwitz.

807
00:40:30.762 --> 00:40:32.138
but elsewhere in places

808
00:40:32.305 --> 00:40:36.309
that are either forgotten
or largely overlooked.

809
00:40:36.309 --> 00:40:39.187
[foreboding music]

810
00:40:40.480 --> 00:40:42.482
- [Tom] The Nazis and
their collaborators

811
00:40:42.482 --> 00:40:45.401
murder six million
Jews during the war.

812
00:40:46.819 --> 00:40:49.614
Millions of others
die at their hands.

813
00:40:52.951 --> 00:40:55.495
But in the face of
unimaginable conditions

814
00:40:55.495 --> 00:40:57.664
and inconceivable cruelty.

815
00:41:01.125 --> 00:41:03.586
some find a way to resist.

816
00:41:05.672 --> 00:41:08.049
- [Rebecca] Resistance takes
so many forms.

817
00:41:08.049 --> 00:41:10.426
There's armed
resistance and uprising.

818
00:41:10.426 --> 00:41:12.804
and that happens
throughout Europe.

819
00:41:12.804 --> 00:41:15.306
There is spiritual resistance.

820
00:41:15.306 --> 00:41:17.642
Just the idea that you
are not going to die

821
00:41:17.642 --> 00:41:19.143
just because the
Nazis want you to.

822
00:41:19.143 --> 00:41:21.145
you are going to try to survive.

823
00:41:21.145 --> 00:41:24.440
You are going to document what
is happening. write letters.

824
00:41:24.440 --> 00:41:27.318
write diaries. bury
them in the ground

825
00:41:27.318 --> 00:41:29.237
so that someday we will be able

826
00:41:29.237 --> 00:41:30.863
to talk about what
you went through.

827
00:41:34.033 --> 00:41:35.201
- [Robert] The nature of tyranny

828
00:41:35.201 --> 00:41:37.245
becomes evident in World War II.

829
00:41:37.245 --> 00:41:39.789
The Nazis and the
Japanese imperialists

830
00:41:39.789 --> 00:41:41.374
are invading their neighbors.

831
00:41:41.374 --> 00:41:43.251
kidnapping. slave laborers.

832
00:41:43.251 --> 00:41:45.753
murdering those who
disagree with them.

833
00:41:46.713 --> 00:41:48.131
It was a natural reaction

834
00:41:48.131 --> 00:41:50.466
on the part of the
occupied peoples

835
00:41:50.466 --> 00:41:53.219
to organize some
form of resistance.

836
00:41:53.219 --> 00:41:55.930
And it happens all over
the world in World War II.

837
00:41:59.600 --> 00:42:02.645
- After surviving
Auschwitz and Buchenwald.

838
00:42:02.645 --> 00:42:05.690
Nobel Laureate Elie Wiesel said:

839
00:42:05.690 --> 00:42:08.151
"We must always take sides.

840
00:42:08.151 --> 00:42:11.362
Neutrality helps the
oppressor. never the victim.

841
00:42:11.362 --> 00:42:16.075
Silence encourages the
tormentor. never the tormented.

842
00:42:16.075 --> 00:42:18.786
Sometimes we must interfere."

843
00:42:20.121 --> 00:42:23.666
In the Pacific. President
Franklin Roosevelt.

844
00:42:23.666 --> 00:42:26.586
General Douglas MacArthur.
and Admiral Chester Nimitz

845
00:42:26.586 --> 00:42:28.963
meet in Pearl Harbor
to draw a plan

846
00:42:28.963 --> 00:42:31.883
for the final defeat
of Imperial Japan.
